# Frequently Asked Questions

## What is college counseling?

College counseling can refer to counseling support for students dealing with emotional, psychological, relational, or adjustment-related concerns during college. Depending on the provider, it may include support for anxiety, stress, depression, relationships, self-esteem, life transitions, or career concerns.

## Can therapy help with college stress?

Therapy can help students explore the factors contributing to their stress and develop coping, communication, emotional regulation, and problem-solving skills. It may be particularly useful when stress is persistent or interfering with everyday life.

## How do I know if I need a therapist for college stress?

Consider seeking professional support if stress is persistent, difficult to manage, or interfering with sleep, concentration, relationships, academic responsibilities, or other areas of everyday life. NIMH recommends professional help when symptoms are severe or distressing, persist, or interfere with daily activities.

## Can a therapist help with academic stress?

Yes. Therapy can address the emotional and psychological factors surrounding academic stress, such as anxiety, perfectionism, fear of failure, low self-esteem, or difficulty coping with pressure. Academic advisors and instructors can provide additional academic-specific support.

## What is the difference between a therapist and an academic advisor?

A therapist focuses on mental and emotional health and may address concerns such as anxiety, depression, stress, relationships, and life transitions. An academic advisor generally focuses on educational planning, degree requirements, course selection, and graduation planning.

## Can college students attend therapy online?

Online therapy may be an option for eligible students who prefer remote sessions or have difficulty traveling to an office. Riaz Counseling currently offers online therapy statewide in Texas.

## Can therapy help with choosing a major or career?

It can help with the emotional and personal side of those decisions, including uncertainty, anxiety, self-esteem, values, and family expectations. Riaz Counseling lists College & Career Guidance as a practice specialization focused on self-discovery and informed decision-making.

## What if my college stress feels overwhelming?

Start by telling someone you trust and consider speaking with a qualified mental health professional. If you are experiencing an immediate mental health emergency or thoughts of suicide or self-harm, seek immediate crisis or emergency assistance rather than relying on an online article.

# You Do Not Have to Manage College Stress Alone

College can involve many competing demands, and feeling stressed does not mean that you are failing.

Sometimes the solution is practical: a better schedule, clearer priorities, more sleep, or asking an advisor for help.

Other times, the stress is connected to something deeper—anxiety, perfectionism, family pressure, loneliness, relationship difficulties, low self-esteem, or uncertainty about the future.

Understanding the difference can help you choose the right kind of support.

If college stress is beginning to interfere with your academic life, relationships, sleep, or overall well-being, speaking with a mental health professional can give you a place to slow down, understand what you are experiencing, and work toward manageable next steps.

